/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069059/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "227ba6a893b014bcf74c8e6d4331bb840af0fc67168c576c6e8605e82c2f1033"} {"question" "q-130455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069059/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea0efa5e94bd77a35d613848d33298e9018559b12d254e117581840a9e7087"} {"question" "q-130455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069059/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea0efa5e94bd77a35d613848d33298e9018559b12d254e117581840a9e7087"} {"question" "q-130455" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069059/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea0efa5e94bd77a35d613848d33298e9018559b12d254e117581840a9e7087"} {"question" "q-130478" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $A,B,$ and $C$ are $7\\times 7$ matrices which of the following statements are always true? \n\n- $(CA)B=C(BA)$\n- $(A+A)B=2AB$\n- $CB=BC$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069059/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea0efa5e94bd77a35d613848d33298e9018559b12d254e117581840a9e7087"} {"question" "q-130478" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's consider the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I  is false. Matrix multiplication is associative and  $(CA)B=C(AB)$ is always true. However,  matrix multiplication is not commutative,   $AB\\ne BA$ in general. Therefore, $(CA)B\\ne C(BA)$ in general.\n\n- Statement II is true. Matrix multiplication is distributive, so $(A+A)B=AB+AB=2AB$ is always true.\n\n- Statement III is false. Matrix multiplication is not commutative in general, so $CB\\ne BC$ in general.\n\nTherefore, only statement II is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069059/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea0efa5e94bd77a35d613848d33298e9018559b12d254e117581840a9e7087"} {"question" "q-130478" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069059/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "227ba6a893b014bcf74c8e6d4331bb840af0fc67168c576c6e8605e82c2f1033"} {"question" "q-130478" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069059/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea0efa5e94bd77a35d613848d33298e9018559b12d254e117581840a9e7087"} {"question" "q-130478" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069059/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea0efa5e94bd77a35d613848d33298e9018559b12d254e117581840a9e7087"} {"question" "q-130478" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069059/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea0efa5e94bd77a35d613848d33298e9018559b12d254e117581840a9e7087"} {"question" "q-275521" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $ABC$, where\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n| $ABC=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069059/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea0efa5e94bd77a35d613848d33298e9018559b12d254e117581840a9e7087"} {"question" "q-275521" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, recall the associative law\n\n$$\nABC=(AB)C=A(BC).\n$$\n\nNotice that $A$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix, $B$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix, and $C$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix. Therefore, \n\n- multiplying $A$ by $B$, we will obtain a $2\\times 1$ matrix, which will require finding $2$ entries, while\n\n- multiplying $B$ by $C$, we will obtain a $3\\times 2$ matrix, which will require finding $6$ entries.\n\nSo, we find the product $AB$ first:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply $AB$ by $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ABC & =(AB)C \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-11 & 11 \\\\ -8 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069059/state.json" "file_sha256" "62ea0efa5e94bd77a35d613848d33298e9018559b12d254e117581840a9e7087"} {"question" "q-275521" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" 